 "productionNotes": "No single second-generation total can be asserted because the year figures themselves conflict. Hagerty's guide gives roughly 96,000 cars for 1968, 89,199 for 1969 and 46,315 for 1970, summing to about 231,500. RM Sotheby's catalogue material states 92,590 for 1968, and Muscle Car Facts gives 49,768 for 1970 with a base/R/T split of 39,431 and 10,337. The 1969 figure of 89,199 is the one number that recurs unchanged across sources. Sub-figures that are reasonably well attested: 20,057 Charger R/T for 1969; 467 1968 hardtops built with the 426 Hemi, of which 211 had the four-speed manual. The 1969 Charger 500 is the least settled number in the model's history - see the notableTrims entry and the disputed claim. The Charger Daytona is generally accepted at approximately 503 street cars, of which about 70 carried the 426 Hemi and the balance the 440 Magnum; a minority of researchers cite 505 once prototypes and duplicated records are counted. No convertible or pillared coupe was offered in this generation; every car is a two-door hardtop.",
